                    sg_hygiene:             `Decision hygiene — separate the deal champion (CEO) from the approver (board/LID) (3 days)`,
                    r2_days_readout:        `Governance days committed:`,
                    r2_days_cap:            `/ 9 days available before the deadline`,
                    r2_friction_label:      `How do you handle Brandt's friction as you spend governance time?`,
                    fric_defend:            `Defend the process to the board — two days of rigor is cheaper than EUR 200M of overpayment`,
                    fric_trim:              `Trim the rituals to the ones Brandt tolerates, keep him onside`,
                    fric_skip:              `Don't antagonize the CEO — keep governance light and move to the bid`,

                    r3_premium_label:       `Set the binding premium over EUR 1.0B standalone (%) — rival opened at 35%, bankers signal 55% “wins it”`,
                    r3_premium_readout:     `Bid premium:`,
                    r3_pct:                 `%`,
                    r3_premium_hint:        `Each 10% premium ≈ EUR 100M. Break-even needs ≈ EUR 120M of recurring synergies; below ~12% you may lose Voltura to the US major; above your walk-away line you are escalating into the winner's curse.`,
                    r3_discipline_label:    `Do you honor the walk-away price you set in Round 2?`,
                    disc_honor:             `Honor it — the pre-committed ceiling is the whole point; if the auction clears it, we walk`,
                    disc_stretch:           `Stretch it modestly — nudge the line up to stay in the auction`,
                    disc_chase:             `Override it — Brandt is right, we are not losing this; clear the rival whatever it takes`,
                    r3_structure_label:     `How do you structure the deal to hedge synergy risk?`,
                    struct_hedged:          `Earn-out + contingent value right + part-stock — sellers share the synergy risk`,
                    struct_some:            `Modest earn-out, mostly cash — a partial hedge`,
                    struct_cash:            `All cash, clean and fast — pay the full premium up front, no contingency`,

                    r4_integration_label:   `Drive the 100-day synergy-capture plan with what intensity?`,
                    integ_disciplined:      `Disciplined integration — clear owners, tracked milestones, realistic ramp to run-rate`,
                    integ_standard:         `Standard PMI — the usual playbook, moderate pace`,
                    integ_rushed:           `Rush to declare victory — book full synergies to the Street in Year 1`,
                    r4_board_label:         `Board & analyst narrative reconciling premium paid to value created.`,
                    board_honest:           `Reconcile premium to realized synergies honestly; flag any impairment risk early and the plan to defend value`,
                    board_optimistic:       `Stay upbeat — reaffirm the strategic logic, defer the hard synergy numbers`,
                    board_declare:          `Declare the deal a win — Voltura is ours, integration is “on track,” match the public commitment Brandt made`,
                    r4_humility_label:      `Calibration: how candidly do you concede where the original analysis was over-optimistic? (%)`,

                    warn_bias_range:        `The number of claims tagged as bias must be between 0 and 5.`,
                    warn_days_over:         `You've committed more than the 9 governance days available before the deadline. Drop a safeguard.`,
                    warn_premium_range:     `The bid premium must be a percentage between 0 and 70.`,
                    warn_humility_range:    `Calibration must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_standalone:    `<strong>Right starting point.</strong> Building up from EUR 1.0B standalone is exactly the discipline the research demands — you valued the asset on its own merits before the anchor could distort it. Now treat the synergy band as the probabilistic forecast it is.`,
                    coach_r1_median:        `<strong>The reference class is a useful corrective</strong> — a ~30% median premium and historically negative average acquirer returns are the base rate. But the cleaner anchor is Voltura's standalone EV: build up from EUR 1.0B, then justify every point of premium with weighted synergies.`,
                    coach_r1_ask:           `<strong>You started from EUR 1.5B and reverse-engineered the synergies to fit.</strong> That's textbook confirmation bias — Pavićević's data says overconfident acquirers do exactly this and earn lower returns. Show me the number you'd reach if you'd never heard “1.5 billion.” What's Voltura worth on its own?`,
                    coach_r1_base:          `<strong>You're capitalising EUR 90M as if it's in the bank.</strong> Your own low case is EUR 45M. If you weight the range, your justified premium drops by a third. Why are you 100% confident in a forecast your own analyst gives a wide band to?`,
                    coach_r1_bull:          `<strong>You've booked the EUR 130M bull case as the central estimate.</strong> That is precisely how the premium gets reverse-engineered to clear the ask. The bull case is a tail, not a plan — capitalise the credible run-rate and treat the planning-fallacy ramp with skepticism.`,
                    coach_r1_lowflags:      `<strong>You logged almost nothing as bias.</strong> The memo is loaded with it — “EUR 1.5B is fair” (anchoring), “strategically essential” (escalation), “never missed a target” (overconfidence), full synergies by Year 2 (planning fallacy). Naming the bias is the first debiasing act; you skipped it.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp valuation discipline.</strong> You anchored to standalone value AND probability-weighted the synergies — the two corrections that stop the ask from setting your number. That's the foundation the safeguards will protect.`,
                    coach_r2_none:          `<strong>You spent zero of your governance budget and went straight to a bid.</strong> Your decision-bias score is now the worst in the room. The 21-day clock is real, but an outside view and a pre-mortem take two days and move your premium by ten points. Which is more expensive — two days, or EUR 200M of overpayment?`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>Some rigor is better than none</strong> — but you left the most powerful corrections on the table. A pre-committed walk-away price and separating the champion from the approver are what actually hold the line when Brandt says “we're not losing this.” Add them before you bid.`,
                    coach_r2_strong:        `<strong>A walk-away price and decision hygiene — the two structural safeguards.</strong> A pre-committed ceiling plus an approver who isn't the champion is what gives the board a real veto, not theatre. Now defend the process so the rituals survive the moment of decision.`,
                    coach_r2_full:          `<strong>That's a debiasing package, defended.</strong> Outside view, pre-mortem, ratified walk-away, red team, champion separated from approver — and you defended the days to the board. This is procedural rationality as an investment, not a delay. Your bias score and your synergy estimate both just improved.`,
                    coach_r3_nowalkaway:    `<strong>You're honoring a walk-away price you never set.</strong> Discipline at the moment of decision only works if you pre-committed a ceiling in Round 2. Without it, “we'll be disciplined” is a hope, not a constraint.`,
                    coach_r3_lost:          `<strong>Disciplined — but you may have lost Voltura.</strong> A bid below the rival's 35% opening risks the one asset Brandt staked his credibility on, and the US major now owns intralogistics. Debiasing isn't always bidding less; was EUR 1.0B your true reservation value, or did you over-correct out of fear of the premium?`,
                    coach_r3_winnerscurse:  `<strong>You won. But you paid a 55%+ premium for a EUR 1.0B asset, and you need EUR 130M of synergies — your bull case — merely to break even.</strong> That's the winner's curse. You won the auction and lost the shareholders. Where was your walk-away number?`,
                    coach_r3_high:          `<strong>You cleared the bankers' “winning” signal.</strong> At 55%+ the premium exceeds the present value of your base-case synergies — value transfers to Voltura's sellers on day one, and only the bull case breaks even. A high bid isn't discipline; it's escalation with a process around it.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>A premium near the reference-class median, with discipline.</strong> You stayed close to the ~30% base rate, kept the bid inside the break-even logic, and didn't let the bankers' signal move you. That's a calibrated number for the right reasons.`,
                    coach_r3_mid:           `<strong>A middling premium.</strong> You're above the disciplined range but below the winner's-curse zone. The question the board will ask: what synergy number justifies this point of premium, and is it your weighted estimate or the ask working backwards?`,
                    coach_r3_allcash:       `<strong>All cash, full premium up front, no contingency.</strong> If synergies disappoint, there's no earn-out or CVR to claw value back — you've handed the sellers the upside and kept the downside. An earn-out or contingent value right is exactly how you hedge a probabilistic synergy forecast.`,
                    coach_r4_lost:          `<strong>You walked, and the rival now owns intralogistics.</strong> Your shareholders weren't diluted by an overpayment — but Brandt's “essential” asset is in a competitor's hands, and the board will ask whether EUR 1.0B was truly your reservation value or a fear-driven over-correction. Calibrated judgment, not reflexive conservatism, was the goal.`,
                    coach_r4_created:       `<strong>This is what the research predicts.</strong> The rigor you installed raised realized synergy capture, the disciplined premium left room for value, and an honest board narrative reconciled price to value. Returns came from synergies exceeding the premium — not from winning the deal.`,
                    coach_r4_curse:         `<strong>The winner's curse, realized.</strong> You won the auction at a premium your synergies couldn't cover; value transferred to the sellers and the acquisition return is negative. The procedural-rationality finding is precisely this: the discipline you skipped is the EUR 200M you paid.`,
                    coach_r4_destroyed:     `<strong>Value destroyed.</strong> Realized synergies didn't exceed the premium you paid, so acquisition return is negative — the classic overpayment outcome. More rigor earlier (a tighter synergy estimate, a lower premium, a hedged structure) is what turns this around.`,
                    coach_r4_mixed:         `<strong>A mixed result.</strong> You captured some synergies but the premium ate most of the value, or the board narrative outran the evidence. The lesson holds: the premium you pay and the rigor you impose, not the “win,” determine the return.`,

